00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:04.710 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  it was the last saturday in september of  2021  as the sky darkened over downtown  toronto a team of drone specialists and  doctors held their breath  nestled inside that black box are human  lungs a priceless life-saving gift from  a donor about to be airlifted for the  first time by drone to a recipient at  toronto general hospital  so this is the roof this is the roof  waiting on the roof that night was dr  shaf khashoggi director of the toronto  lung transplant program so you've got  all those checks and balances in place  right you've tested it and tested it how  did you actually feel at night  in nervous because you know like in an  operating room i can be in control and  an understanding and and respond to  everything i'm trained to respond to it  with a drone  hovering over your head like  i  can't do anything about it out of your  control it's out of my control it's life  that you're transporting in that little  box yeah yeah no no it is  the journey took just six minutes and  though they'd done more than 50 test  flights with a dummy payload there were  still fears the gps might fail or a bird  might attack we were in contact with  them and we knew it would take on but we  couldn't see it right we're standing  there waiting waiting waiting then what  happens is it comes over toronto general  and it kind of stops and hovers and it's  probably only three or four seconds but  it but it seemed like an age like i'm  thinking like  just come down you know  several floors below waiting in a  hospital bed was 63 year old ellen odak  in  an hour or two hours they're going to  fix my lungs  i was really at the end of the line i  was on major quantities of oxygen just  to get up just to walk around his lungs  scarred by pulmonary fibrosis were  operating at less than 10 percent  capacity when the lungs are shot and  they're not going to get better the  writing is on the wall  he had weeks to live  dr khashoggi and his team were pushing  the limits doing something that had  never been done before and needed  consent from the potential recipient  they came to the room in the afternoon  and they said we've got this project  that's transporting by drones are you  interested in participating they  describe the risk of it  and i said  i'm a technical guy i'm an engineer by  profession  i said advancement of science sure i'll  participate  his life was hanging in the balance  dependent on this delivery  we're just  unhooking the lung from the from the  drone right now as we speak hope you  have some champagne there  and it was no ordinary drone it was  specially designed by uniter  bioelectronics in bromont quebec to have  multiple backup systems and we built in  a ballistic parachute so with two  engines ballistic parachutes right so if  the if the the drone drops too fast tilt  suddenly loses power it will cut all the  engines a parachute will fire and the  drone will come down to the ground  safely and it was very exciting because  like drone guys are like cool engineer  dudes and they can do all that stuff but  they didn't sort of think like it's a  line well gave a drone crashes i'll just  get another one well no  it's got a lung in it that's a person  that's someone's life  how far was it flying that night but  it's kind of like the kitty hawk right  it's like the first flight it is the  first flight and actually the most  difficult part of drone flight like if  we can fly in the middle of downtown  toronto safely we can fly anywhere in  north america it is another step in dr  khashoggi's grand plan to streamline the  complex logistics of organ transplants  they're the most fragile and the most  challenging of the major organs so  kidneys were transplanted first then the  heart and liver and lungs lagged behind  for almost two decades  lung transplants are his life's work  he's been focused on them since medical  school in the 1980s and i was very  fortunate that when i was training here  at the university of toronto the first  successful lung transplant in the world  was being performed right here at  toronto general hospital it was quite  amazing but it also you know as a young  student hit me that wow it really is a  miracle but it's extremely risky it  doesn't really work that often that was  39 years ago this is the air being  reintroduced into the new lung and it  was the moment dr khashoggi decided to  devote himself to lungs making multiple  improvements steadily increasing the  number of successful lung transplants  good evening everybody  thank you for having me here  here he is at a ted med talk in san  diego in 2010 i've done hundreds of lung  transplants and yet every single time i  do that operation it i marvel at what we  do he invented a technique to keep donor  lungs alive outside the body so they  could be evaluated even treated made  healthier and tailored to the recipient  we set out to say  can we genetically modify organs so that  they're better  prepared to deal with the stress of a  transplant  it's called ex-vivo lung perfusion or  evlp and this lung is alive so while  it's sitting here it's not dying anymore  we're facilitating recovery  what are you doing  it's interesting versions of the  invention are now being used around the  world that's when we showed you could  keep a lung for 12 hours outside the  body at 37 degrees with no deterioration  and function remember i told you lung  cells start dying in 20 minutes so that  was remarkable like when we did that we  knew we'd hit a home run in this process  how long now can you keep a lung  functioning and healthy outside the body  we're looking at taking lungs to 36  hours to 72 hours outside the body i  mean that means you know you get along  on friday you can book it book it for  monday morning and not only a lung  that's deteriorated over time but  actually could be made better by monday  morning he envisions one day having lung  hospitals where donor organs would be  taken to be treated and healed and  prepared for the specific recipient  ultimately really the new frontier will  be can we modify it so that it looks  more like you more like self so you  won't reject it can we use your own stem  cells to make a lung that is you the  other new frontier is in the sky  uniter bioelectronics is developing  autonomous airborne organ delivery  systems  drones that can fly manned or unmanned  within congested cities or even hundreds  of kilometers between cities  when did you connect the dots between  drones and organ donation sometimes the  barrier to transplant is just logistics  can be as much as we can't get a plane  or there's a snowstorm or there's we  don't have staff or the operating rooms  are busy well if we can now have a  system where we don't have an operating  room we put the lung on evlp when  there's a room and a surgeon ready we do  a lung transplant so we can now do five  lung transplants in a day five patients  we used to do one a day right so it's  looking at logistics like that so the  next thing was the the transportation of  organs this was the first lung delivery  by drone but other medical teams have  done similar tests with different organs  i'm pretty nervous let's not screw it  in 2019 in baltimore maryland we're for  launch let's make some history  this drone carried a human kidney  getting a little breezy up here from a  recipient to a donor  another successful test that it's hoped  will one day make oregon transport  simpler and less expensive you have a  plane  to take the team to go pick up the  organs and come back but you have five  different teams you have lung liver  kidney heart teams all going so often  one donor hospital will have five teams  and five planes coming from five  different places pick up their organ and  go back to their hospital and find more  plane trips  just to transport an organ that weighs  two kilograms  when i was here last i was pushing him  in a wheelchair la odak is back on his  feet breathing easy and he's just  welcomed his first grandchild and is  looking forward to more i was given a  second life  really that's what it is basically is  thanks to a generous donor i have now a  second life the the lucky coincidence  was he's like an engineer that just  thought this was the coolest thing that  ever could have happened yeah and he's  doing amazingly well and he calls what's  happened to him a second life it is it  is because you know i mean people live  on the lung transplant list knowing that  one in five of them will die before they  can get along in time by constantly  innovating it's our best attempt  currently to mimic what's inside the  body dr kashaf ji's vision is that organ  transplants will one day be as routine  and commonplace as hip replacements i  can't see  hundreds of drones flying around yet  like how's that going to happen how's it  going to be safe you know i think  there's going to likely need to be lanes  in the sky of of of where drones go and  i think all of that needs to be figured  out but you know organ transplantation  is probably  a pretty important reason to really  figure that out  rather than delivering your laundry  detergent  i think most people would agree  [Music]

